\title{CS-137 Nucleonic Level Sensor Operation}
\author{John Doe}
\date{Last Updated: 04/2023}
\maketitle
\section*{Introduction}
The CS-137 nuclear level sensor measures the concentration of $^{137}$Cs-131 in aqueous samples.

\section*{Conceptual Overview}
The device consists of a linear fission reactor (liquid scintillator), a scintillation counter (photomultiplier tube), and a readout amplifier.

\section*{Sensor Principle}
$^{137}$Cs-131 decays via $\beta^-$ emission with a half-life of 2.4 days. A cesium-137 atom captures a neutron to become a stable $^{137}$Cs-130 atom, releasing a proton. The $^{137}$Cs-130 atom emits $\beta^-$ and becomes a stable $^{137}$Cs-131 atom.

\section*{Sensitivity Considerations}
Typical limits of detection are:
- Background count < 10^10 counts/sec
- Background < 10^15 counts/sec
- Signal-to-noise ratio ≥ 3

\section*{Sensor Calibration}
Calibration requires a reference material (typically graphite or CaF$_2$) whose activity is known. During calibration, the sensor is loaded with the reference, exposed to decay modes, and calibrated against a known standard.

\section*{Data Collection}
Data acquisition occurs via a second photomultiplier tube (PMT) in a coincidence geometry. The detector reads the peak height of the $^{137}$Cs-131 signal relative to the background.

\section*{Safety Considerations}
Uses require PPE including HEPA filtration, lead shielding, and appropriate ventilation. Dispose of used equipment according to local regulations.

