\section*{Abstract}
We analyze radiation-enhanced reflector assemblies to improve device performance in vacuum environments. We investigate how changes in surface material, geometry, and material ratios affect radiation attenuation and gain.

\section*{1. Introduction}
\subsection*{Background}
Current vacuum technology relies on high-power lasers and electron beams. High-power lasers increase beam size, while electron beams have uniform cross-section. Radiation-enhanced structures use additional layers to absorb, scatter, or convert X-rays to thermal energy or photonic energy.

\subsection*{Use Cases}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{High-power density ablation (HPD)}: Using electron beams to heat metal targets for welding or cutting.
\item \textbf{Backscattering diagnostics (BSD)}: Measuring material thickness and density with X-rays.
\item \textbf{Photonic heating (PH)}: Using optical emission to absorb or convert photons to heat.
\item \textbf{High-intensity focusing}: Deploying large mirrors or lenses using focused radiation.
\end{itemize}
\section*{2. Methodology}
\subsection*{Methodology Overview}
\begin{itemize}
